      "## List the systems the shift depends on",
      "A shift-start check begins with the systems the specialist will actually use during the shift. List the queue, the phone or chat tool, the shared drive, and the reporting sheet by name. Include the login that proves access rather than the bookmark that opens a page. A check of things the person does not need is wasted effort.",
      "Separate a hard dependency from a convenience. Without the queue, the shift cannot start, while a reports tab can wait until later in the morning. Mark each item as blocking or non-blocking. The order of the check should follow that distinction.",
      "Name the owner of each system and the support path when it fails. A specialist who cannot reach the queue needs to know who to contact before the first customer writes in. Put the contact in the check, not in a separate directory. The person should not search for help during a live problem.",
      "Review the list when the work changes. A new tool, a new client, or a changed permission can add a dependency that the old check did not cover. Update the list with the effective date. An outdated check gives false confidence.",
      "## Order the check from access to evidence",
      "Start with identity and access because everything else depends on them. Confirm the login works, the multi-factor prompt completes, and the correct workspace opens. Then check the queue and the tools that hold live work. Save reports and reference files for the end.",
      "Record the time each check completes. A login that takes twenty minutes is a finding even if it eventually works. The timestamp shows whether the shift truly started on time. It also helps compare one day with another.",
      "Use a single short form or a fixed message in the team channel. The format should take less than ten minutes and leave a visible result. A check that requires a long narrative will be skipped on busy mornings. Keep the fields stable so the history can be compared.",
      "Finish with one line that states ready, ready with exceptions, or blocked. The line should name the exception and the owner. A status that says fine without evidence cannot be reviewed. The supervisor should see the result without opening five tools.",
      "## Record a failed check as a work item",
      "A failed check is work, not a note in a chat. Create an item with the system, the symptom, the time, and the person affected. Assign an owner and a next action. The shift should not proceed as if the dependency were healthy.",
      "Distinguish a failure the specialist can fix from one that needs an administrator. A wrong password may be a reset, while a missing role needs a ticket. Record which path was used and when. This keeps the specialist from improvising access.",
      "Set a rule for starting work when a non-blocking check fails. The specialist may proceed with the queue while the report tool is repaired. Write the rule so the boundary is not renegotiated each time. The client should know which work can continue.",
      "Close the item only when the check passes on a later attempt. A workaround is not a repair unless the owner accepts it. Record the evidence and the date. The next shift needs to know whether the problem is really gone.",
      "## Keep the check short enough to repeat",
      "A check that takes half the shift will not survive a busy week. Limit it to the systems that block the first task and the few that fail often. Move everything else to a weekly review. Speed is part of the design.",
      "Combine steps where the tool allows it. One login can confirm identity, workspace, and queue access if the screens are linked. Do not add a second confirmation for the same fact. The check should test the dependency, not the person's patience.",
      "Publish the check where the shift begins, such as the top of the queue or the team channel. A document buried in a folder will be forgotten. Keep the latest version and remove old copies. The specialist should not have to choose which version to follow.",
      "Ask the team after two weeks whether any step is redundant. The people doing the check see the friction first. Remove one step at a time and watch for missed failures. A shorter check that still catches problems is the goal.",
      "## Review failures for patterns",
      "Review failures weekly by system, cause, and time lost. One flaky login may be a password policy, while several failures at the same hour may be a maintenance window. Group the causes before choosing a fix. A single dramatic failure can hide a boring pattern.",
      "Share the pattern with the system owner, not only the specialist. A check that repeatedly catches the same missing role points to an onboarding gap. The repair belongs where the permission is granted. The desk can only report what it sees.",
      "Measure how often a shift starts blocked and how long it takes to recover. A rising blocked rate is a service risk even when the team works hard. Use the number in the client review. It makes an invisible dependency visible.",
      "Retest the check after a tool change or a new client launch. Run it as if the shift were starting for the first time. Record the result and the date. A check that is never retested becomes a ritual rather than a control."
